# EL-2 — inbound EchoLink audio across a talkspurt discontinuity.
#
# Provenance (LP-1, `Tests/FIXTURES.md`): a capture of the maintainer's own
# QSO with *ECHOTEST*, 2026-08-12. Cited by SHA-256, never by path.
#
# Why this capture and not the richer one: *ECHOTEST* is EchoLink's own public
# test conference, so this session has exactly two parties — us and a public
# service. The other two captures carry four individual operators, and the
# proxy header embeds the peer's IPv4 address in every 0x05 and 0x06 frame,
# with no way to omit it that does not mean editing octets. So an audio fixture
# from those captures would put another operator's address in the repository.
# This one puts a published service address there instead, which is the least
# that can be disclosed while still having audio evidence at all.
#
# On alignment: this capture has no TCP SYN, so `--assume-aligned` is required
# and the recipe below carries it. The alignment is not assumed on faith — it
# is established three ways: the stream decodes to 432 frames with zero bytes
# left over, which a wrong header size or endianness does not survive for more
# than a few frames; every frame's type, peer and length are self-consistent;
# and the RTP sequence numbers run consecutively across hundreds of frames.
#
# What this fixture pins, all of it contrary to a plain reading of RFC 3550:
#
#  * The RTP version bits are 3, not 2. A parser written faithfully from the
#    RFC rejects every one of these packets. Accept 3; do not "fix" it, and do
#    not reject 2 either.
#  * The timestamp is 0 in every packet, and never advances. This is a protocol
#    property, seen across four independent peers in the wider capture set, not
#    one client's quirk. `JitterBuffer` keys on timestamps, so it has nothing
#    to key on here — EL-7 must synthesise the clock from the sequence number.
#  * Each packet is 144 bytes: a 12-byte header and four 33-byte GSM 06.10
#    frames, i.e. 80 ms in four 20 ms units.
#
# The cut spans the discontinuity deliberately. Frames [152]..[157] are the end
# of ECHOTEST's welcome announcement, sequence 146..151; frames [203]..[210]
# are the start of our own audio being echoed back, sequence 0..7. Sequence
# runs backwards across that boundary. It is a new talkspurt, not reordering
# and not a wrap, and it is the case an expander that counts from zero gets
# wrong. The gap in the `[n]` indices is the frames left out between them.
# Machine-generated. The captures themselves are not in the repository —
# they are the maintainer's own, and large. Regenerate with:
#   scripts/pcap-to-fixture.py <capture> --transport tcp --stream 0 --dir in --range 152:157,203:210 --assume-aligned
# Capture SHA-256: 2a8aa1ba78218170fcf3cf4c7a45c6c67fd3541192cdab895307f6a16699cd01
#
# Each line is one EchoLink proxy frame verbatim, reassembled from the
# TCP stream: the 9-byte header — type(1), peer IPv4(4), length(4,
# LITTLE-endian) — and its payload. `[n]` is the frame's index in
# arrival order across both directions, so a gap in the indices is a
# frame deliberately left out.

# [152] 14.112s <== UDP DATA    peer 13.57.14.183   144B  RTP v3 pt3 seq   146 ts 0 ssrc 0  4 GSM
